      "url": "https://hedcycling.com/blogs/tech-performance/aerodynamics-bike-wheels",
      "snippet": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"df9438040b21cdff\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHubs and bearings: A quality rear hub with smooth cartridge or ceramic bearings rolls with less friction. Good internals also give quick degree engagement, so power reaches the road the instant you push\n Spokes: Bladed or carbon spokes cut their own small slice of drag\n Build quality:A hand-built wheel with even spoke tension holds true and resists the stress that turns a small manufacturing defect into a failure. This is where craft shows up as excellent durability [...] On flat, fast roads and in the time trial: deeper rims pay off, since aerodynamic drag is your main enemy at high speed\n On climbs and rolling terrain:shallower, lighter wheels handle better and the aero penalty is small\n In wind: a stable mid-depth wheel beats a deep wheel you are fighting to hold straight [...] A wheel is aerodynamic when its shape manages airflow cleanly at speed. As the wheel moves, air has to split around the rim and the tire and rejoin behind them. Turbulence in that process is aerodynamic drag, and drag is what costs you speed. Because drag climbs with the square of your speed, the faster you ride, the more your wheels matter.\n\nThree things drive a wheel's aerodynamic performance:\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"df9438040b21cdff\">>>",
