      "snippet": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d693da828eec48a7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe Short Answer: The Coefficient of Rolling Resistance (Crr) is a dimensionless number typically in the range of 0.002–0.007 for road bike tires. The lower the Crr, the faster the tire rolls. It's the energy lost as your tire deforms and recovers with each wheel rotation.\n\n## What Is Rolling Resistance, and Why Does It Matter? [...] the forces acting on a cyclist are power, gravity, aerodynamic drag and rolling resistance\n\nThe physics are captured in the Coefficient of Rolling Resistance (Crr), a dimensionless number typically in the range of 0.002–0.007 for road bike tires. The lower the Crr, the faster the tire rolls. At race speeds, rolling resistance typically accounts for 10–20% of total power demand — comparable in scale to aerodynamic drag at lower speeds, and far more significant than most riders realize. [...] | Tire / Setup | Estimated Crr | Estimated Time (Kona course, 200W) |\n --- \n| Continental Gatorskin (training tire) | 0.0060 | 5:16:00 |\n| Continental GP 5000 (clincher, butyl) | 0.0040 | 5:08:00 |\n| Continental GP 5000 S TR (tubeless) | 0.0031 | 5:03:30 |\n| Vittoria Corsa Pro Speed TLR (tubeless) | 0.0022 | 4:59:00 |\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d693da828eec48a7\">>>",
