Forces at Work

    For a bicycle moving at a constant velocity, there are three signi辌ant re-
tarding forces (Figure 1):

rolling resistance, due to contact between the tires and road;

gravitational resistance, if the road is sloped; and

air resistance, usually the largest of the three.

When accelerating, the rider also uses energy to overcome translational and
rotational inertia, although the model does not take these into account.
